{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/spicy-curry-how-it-traveled-and-changed-in-japan/audio/main-2d12ad89262f.mp3","durationSec":353.472,"cues":[{"start":0.02,"end":2.96,"text":"“Curry” does not describe one fixed recipe."},{"start":3.78,"end":11.52,"text":"Across South Asia and India, different regions have developed many local dishes using their own ingredients and spice combinations."},{"start":12.28,"end":19.2,"text":"Later, Britain produced commercial curry powder: a prepared mixture that gave cooks a more regular result."},{"start":19.88,"end":25.78,"text":"This mixture eventually reached Japan, where cooks changed the sauce to suit rice and local meals."},{"start":26.38,"end":31.5,"text":"We can understand this journey through three ideas: **source**, **change**, and **result**."},{"start":31.5,"end":34.28,"text":"The source is an earlier food tradition."},{"start":35,"end":38.76,"text":"Change happens when cooks meet new ingredients, needs, or tastes."},{"start":39.5,"end":42.76,"text":"The result is a new style that has its own identity."},{"start":43.98,"end":49.82,"text":"British colonial contact with South Asia introduced British people to many spiced dishes."},{"start":50.48,"end":54.84,"text":"However, those dishes did not form one simple system called curry."},{"start":55.44,"end":58.38,"text":"They came from different places and cooking traditions."},{"start":58.38,"end":65.18,"text":"In Britain, commercial curry powder turned several spices into a steady, convenient mixture."},{"start":65.94,"end":67.34,"text":"This was an important change."},{"start":67.96,"end":73,"text":"A prepared powder could travel, be stored, and give similar results in different kitchens."},{"start":73.7,"end":79.62,"text":"At the same time, it reduced a wide range of regional dishes to a more general idea of “curry.”"},{"start":80.52,"end":89.54,"text":"Using our lens, South Asian food traditions were the source, commercial powder was the change, and a portable British curry style was the result."},{"start":89.54,"end":94.04,"text":"Commercial curry powder entered Japan in the early Meiji period."},{"start":94.04,"end":97.96,"text":"Japanese cooks thickened the sauce and served it with rice."},{"start":97.96,"end":102.84,"text":"Curry then became popular in homes, towns, and school lunches."},{"start":103.7,"end":106.94,"text":"One theory connects this development with the Japanese Navy."},{"start":107.94,"end":117.68,"text":"A British Royal Navy curry-flavored stew was adapted into a thick sauce that worked with rice and offered a practical meal containing meat and vegetables."},{"start":118.64,"end":121.66,"text":"Retired sailors later helped spread this naval style."},{"start":121.66,"end":125.98,"text":"Japanese companies also made curry powder easier to obtain."},{"start":126.9,"end":133.1,"text":"Minejiro Yamazaki produced Japan’s first domestically made curry powder in 1923."},{"start":133.98,"end":141.2,"text":"In 1930, Higashiya, the company before S&B Foods, launched curry powder for home use."},{"start":141.2,"end":144.66,"text":"A curry blend does not depend on chili alone."},{"start":145.62,"end":161.08,"text":"S&B Foods lists ingredients including turmeric, coriander, fenugreek, cumin, pepper, chili pepper, cinnamon, fennel, ginger, star anise, cloves, nutmeg, cardamom, and herbs."},{"start":161.88,"end":165.44,"text":"Together, they balance flavor, color, and spiciness."},{"start":166.2,"end":168.54,"text":"Different spices make different contributions."},{"start":168.54,"end":174.12,"text":"Chili pepper can create strong heat, while turmeric gives a noticeable color."},{"start":174.12,"end":178.76,"text":"Cinnamon, cloves, and cardamom can add warm aromas."},{"start":179.46,"end":186.78,"text":"The final experience depends on the relationship between all the ingredients, not simply on how much chili is present."},{"start":187.48,"end":196.92,"text":"This returns us to the idea of change: when cooks adjust one part of the blend, the result can smell, taste, and feel quite different."},{"start":196.92,"end":201.16,"text":"The heat of chili is not the same as the physical temperature of food."},{"start":201.92,"end":205.06,"text":"Chili peppers contain a compound called **capsaicin**."},{"start":205.8,"end":209.88,"text":"Capsaicin activates a receptor in the body called TRPV1."},{"start":210.42,"end":219.32,"text":"This receptor also responds to real heat, so the brain can receive a heat-like signal even when the food itself is not unusually hot."},{"start":219.88,"end":223.8,"text":"That is why spicy curry can create a burning sensation in the mouth."},{"start":223.8,"end":230.44,"text":"The experience may continue after you swallow because the body is still responding to capsaicin."},{"start":231.18,"end":240.8,"text":"In everyday conversation, people often describe curry as “hot,” but this word can mean two things: high physical temperature or strong chili heat."},{"start":241.28,"end":243.86,"text":"The two sensations can also happen together."},{"start":244.6,"end":254.58,"text":"A common Japanese home curry has a thick, rich sauce with ingredients such as onions, carrots, potatoes, and meat, and it is usually served with rice."},{"start":254.58,"end":257.94,"text":"Curry roux blocks make this style convenient."},{"start":258.68,"end":266.5,"text":"They contain flour, oil, seasonings, spices, and other ingredients, so they help thicken and flavor the whole pot."},{"start":267.24,"end":269.72,"text":"This texture changes the eating experience."},{"start":270.46,"end":277.5,"text":"A thick sauce covers the rice and vegetables, and each bite combines sauce, staple food, and solid ingredients."},{"start":277.5,"end":284.66,"text":"The spice blend may provide aroma, color, and heat, but the body of the sauce is also important."},{"start":285.4,"end":291.84,"text":"Because there is no single South Asian curry standard, “different from curry abroad” can mean many things."},{"start":292.44,"end":295.08,"text":"The comparison depends on the particular dish."},{"start":295.84,"end":301.24,"text":"Japanese curry shows how adaptation can create something both international and local."},{"start":301.98,"end":307.5,"text":"Its sources include varied South Asian food traditions and British commercial curry powder."},{"start":307.5,"end":318.24,"text":"Important changes in Japan included a thicker sauce, service with rice, practical meat-and-vegetable meals, and convenient products for home cooking."},{"start":318.96,"end":322.24,"text":"The result became familiar in homes and school lunches."},{"start":322.92,"end":327.3,"text":"This history does not mean that Japanese curry is an incorrect copy."},{"start":328.14,"end":333.56,"text":"Through these changes, it became a familiar part of home cooking and school lunches in Japan."},{"start":333.56,"end":340.04,"text":"The source–change–result lens also reminds us not to treat all curry as one dish."},{"start":340.8,"end":347.24,"text":"When we compare curries, we can ask more precise questions: Which region or recipe is the source?"},{"start":347.88,"end":348.62,"text":"What changed?"},{"start":349.28,"end":352.82,"text":"What sensory or cultural result did that change produce?"}],"words":[{"text":"Curry","start":0.02,"end":0.56},{"text":"does","start":0.56,"end":0.82},{"text":"not","start":0.82,"end":1.16},{"text":"describe","start":1.16,"end":1.64},{"text":"one","start":1.64,"end":2.04},{"text":"fixed","start":2.04,"end":2.4},{"text":"recipe","start":2.4,"end":2.96},{"text":"Across","start":3.78,"end":3.82},{"text":"South","start":3.82,"end":4.12},{"text":"Asia","start":4.12,"end":4.54},{"text":"and","start":4.54,"end":4.98},{"text":"India","start":4.98,"end":5.38},{"text":"different","start":5.76,"end":6.1},{"text":"regions","start":6.1,"end":6.58},{"text":"have","start":6.58,"end":6.88},{"text":"developed","start":6.88,"end":7.28},{"text":"many","start":7.28,"